    "subtitle": "Price Support Mechanism, Cost of Cultivation Methodology, Swaminathan Commission & Statutory FRP for Sugarcane",
    "overview": "Minimum Support Price (MSP) is an agricultural price floor announced by the Government of India prior to the sowing season to protect agricultural producers from sharp drops in market prices. Recommended by the Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP) under the Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare, MSP currently covers 22 mandated crops (plus Fair Remunerative Price for Sugarcane under the Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966). The methodology relies on comprehensive cost concepts: A2 (paid-out costs), A2+FL (paid out plus imputed family labour), and comprehensive C2 (including rental value of owned land and interest on fixed capital). Understanding the institutional framework of CACP, the National Commission on Farmers (M.S. Swaminathan Commission 2004-06) recommendation of 50% over C2, and the Union Budget 2018-19 benchmark of 1.5 times A2+FL is critical for UP PCS-J Paper I GK.",
    "summary": "MSP is a non-statutory executive price support policy (except FRP for sugarcane which has statutory backing under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955). CACP recommends prices based on domestic demand-supply, international parity, cost of production, and inter-crop parity. While CACP recommends, the final decision is approved by the Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) chaired by the Prime Minister.",
    "fullNotes": "### 1. Institutional Framework: CACP & CCEA\n- **CACP (Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices)**: Established in 1965 as Agricultural Prices Commission (renamed CACP in 1985). It is an attached statutory advisory body of the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare. It comprises a Chairman, Member Secretary, one official member, and two non-official members (farmers' representatives).\n- **CCEA (Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs)**: The apex cabinet body headed by the Prime Minister that accords final executive approval to MSPs for Kharif, Rabi, and other commercial crops.\n\n### 2. Crops Mandated under MSP Regime (Total 22 + 1 Statutory FRP)\n- **7 Cereals**: Paddy, Wheat, Maize, Sorghum (Jowar), Pearl Millet (Bajra), Barley, Ragi.\n- **5 Pulses**: Gram, Tur (Arhar), Moong, Urad, Lentil (Masur).\n- **7 Oilseeds**: Groundnut, Rapeseed-Mustard, Soybean, Sesamum, Sunflower, Safflower, Nigerseed.\n- **4 Commercial Crops**: Copra, Sugarcane (FRP), Cotton, Raw Jute.\n- *Note*: MSP for Toria and De-husked Coconut are fixed on the basis of MSPs of Rapeseed-Mustard and Copra respectively.\n\n### 3. Cost Concepts Used by CACP\n- **A2 Cost**: Covers all paid-out expenses incurred directly by the farmer in cash and kind (seeds, fertilizers, pesticides, hired human/bullock/machine labour, fuel, irrigation, interest on working capital).\n- **A2 + FL Cost**: Covers A2 plus an imputed economic valuation of unpaid family labour deployed in cultivation.\n- **C2 Cost (Comprehensive Cost)**: A comprehensive economic cost comprising A2 + FL plus imputed rent and interest on owned land and fixed capital assets.\n- **M.S. Swaminathan Commission Recommendation (2006)**: Recommended that MSP should be fixed at a minimum of 50% above the comprehensive cost of production (C2 + 50%).\n- **Union Budget 2018-19 Policy Announcement**: The Central Government benchmarked MSP at a minimum of 150% (50% profit margin) over the **A2 + FL** cost of production across all mandated Kharif and Rabi crops.\n\n### 4. Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) vs State Advised Price (SAP)\n- **FRP for Sugarcane**: Governed statutorily by the Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966 issued under Section 3 of the Essential Commodities Act, 1955. Sugar mills are statutorily bound to pay FRP to cane growers within 14 days of delivery, failing which 15% interest per annum is chargeable.\n- **SAP (State Advised Price)**: States like Uttar Pradesh, Punjab, and Haryana announce SAP, which is traditionally higher than the central FRP, leading to historic judicial conflicts resolved by the Supreme Court (e.g., *UP Coop. Cane Unions Federations v. West UP Sugar Mills Assn.* [2004] affirming state power to fix SAP).",
