    "detailedExplanation": "### 1. Food Corporation of India (FCI)\n- **Statutory Origin**: Created under the **Food Corporations Act, 1964** following the L.K. Jha Committee recommendations on food management.\n- **Core Functions**: (i) Effective price support operations at MSP to safeguard farmer interests; (ii) Distributing foodgrains throughout the country for Public Distribution System (PDS); (iii) Maintaining operational and buffer stocks of foodgrains to ensure national food security.\n- **Economic Cost of FCI**: Comprises three components: (a) MSP (including state taxes/levies/mandi cess); (b) Procurement incidentals (mandi labour, bagging, transport to godown); (c) Distribution cost (freight, handling, transit/storage losses, storage charges paid to Central Warehousing Corporation [CWC] and state warehousing corporations).\n- **Food Subsidy**: Difference between the Economic Cost of foodgrains incurred by FCI and the Central Issue Price (CIP) charged to states/beneficiaries under NFSA.\n\n### 2. National Food Security Act (NFSA), 2013\n- **Enactment Date**: Signed into law on September 10, 2013 (retrospectively effective July 5, 2013).\n- **Population Coverage (Section 3)**: Legally covers up to **75% of the rural population** and **50% of the urban population**, determined using 2011 Census figures (benefiting roughly 81.35 crore citizens).\n- **Beneficiary Categories**:\n  1. **Antyodaya Anna Yojana (AAY)**: Poorest of the poor households, entitled to **35 kg of foodgrains per household per month**.\n  2. **Priority Households (PHH)**: Identified by state guidelines, entitled to **5 kg of foodgrains per person per month**.\n- **Women Empowerment Provision (Section 13)**: The eldest woman in the household, not less than **18 years of age**, shall be the head of the household for the purpose of issuing ration cards. If no woman is 18+, the eldest male is head until a female member reaches 18.\n- **Maternity Benefit (Section 4)**: Pregnant women and lactating mothers are entitled to free meals during pregnancy and 6 months after childbirth through Anganwadi centres, plus a cash maternity benefit of not less than Rs. 6,000 (operationalized via PM Matru Vandana Yojana).\n- **Food Security Allowance (Section 8)**: If mandated foodgrains are not supplied, eligible beneficiaries are statutorily entitled to receive a cash Food Security Allowance from the State Government.\n\n### 3. Buffer Stock Norms & Decentralized Procurement (DCP)\n- **Buffer Stock Norms**: Prescribed quarterly foodgrain inventory targets (April 1, July 1, October 1, January 1) comprising operational stocks for current distribution and strategic reserves for emergency crop failure or war.\n- **Decentralized Procurement (DCP) Scheme**: Introduced in 1997-98, where State Governments procure, store, and distribute foodgrains locally under NFSA, minimizing unnecessary inter-state railway transit costs.",
