    "subjectId": "gk-economy",
    "introduction": "Rural agricultural finance in India transitioned from the grip of non-institutional moneylenders to a multi-agency institutional credit structure comprising Commercial Banks, Regional Rural Banks (RRBs), and Cooperative Credit Societies. At the institutional helm stands the National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD), established in 1982 on the recommendations of the B. Sivaraman Committee (CRAFICARD). Credit delivery was revolutionized through the Kisan Credit Card (KCC) scheme (1998, R.V. Gupta Committee) and RBI's Priority Sector Lending (PSL) norms requiring scheduled commercial banks to allocate 40% of Adjusted Net Bank Credit (ANBC) to priority sectors (with 18% earmarked for agriculture). In 2019, direct unconditional cash income support was introduced through the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM-KISAN).",
    "detailedExplanation": "### 1. NABARD: Origin, Structure & Functions\n- **Establishment**: Set up on **July 12, 1982** under the **National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development Act, 1981 (Act No. 61 of 1981)**.\n- **Committee**: Recommended by the **Committee to Review Arrangements for Institutional Credit for Agriculture and Rural Development (CRAFICARD)** chaired by **B. Sivaraman** (former Member, Planning Commission).\n- **Capital & Ownership**: Wholly owned by the Government of India (post-2018 amendment which acquired RBI's remaining 0.4% stake).\n- **Core Functions**:\n  1. **Apex Refinancing**: Provides short, medium, and long-term refinance to State Cooperative Banks, RRBs, and commercial banks for agricultural and rural projects.\n  2. **Rural Infrastructure Development Fund (RIDF)**: Created in 1995-96 in NABARD from PSL shortfall deposits of commercial banks to finance state rural infrastructure projects (rural roads, irrigation, bridges).\n  3. **Supervision**: Exercises statutory supervisory inspection of State Cooperative Banks (StCBs), District Central Cooperative Banks (DCCBs), and RRBs under Section 35(6) of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949.\n  4. **Self-Help Group (SHG) Bank Linkage Programme**: Pioneered in 1992, becoming the world's largest microfinance movement.\n\n### 2. Kisan Credit Card (KCC) Scheme\n- **Launch Year**: Introduced in **August 1998** based on the model scheme formulated by the **R.V. Gupta Committee** on agricultural credit.\n- **Objective**: To provide adequate, timely, and cost-effective credit from the formal banking system to farmers for their cultivation expenses, post-harvest expenses, produce marketing, consumption requirements, and maintenance of farm assets.\n- **Expansion (2019)**: Facility extended to animal husbandry, dairy, and fisheries farmers for their working capital requirements.\n- **Insurance & Validity**: Valid for 5 years subject to annual renewal. Comes with RuPay debit card enabled with ATM and POS transactions, alongside personal accident insurance.\n\n### 3. Priority Sector Lending (PSL) Targets for Agriculture\n- **RBI Mandate**: Domestic Scheduled Commercial Banks and Foreign Banks (with 20+ branches) must allocate **40% of Adjusted Net Bank Credit (ANBC)** or Credit Equivalent of Off-Balance Sheet Exposure (CEOBE) to Priority Sectors.\n- **Agriculture Sub-target**: **18% of ANBC**, within which a dedicated **8% sub-target** (gradually stepping up to 10%) is prescribed for **Small and Marginal Farmers (SMFs)**.\n- **Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) & Small Finance Banks (SFBs)**: Mandated to achieve **75% of ANBC** as Priority Sector Lending.\n- **PSL Certificates (PSLCs)**: Tradable instruments allowing banks with surplus priority lending to sell certificates to banks facing shortfalls without actual transfer of loan assets.\n\n### 4. Direct Income Support: PM-KISAN\n- **Launch Date**: Effective from **December 1, 2018** (announced in interim Budget February 2019).\n- **Nature**: Central Sector Scheme with 100% funding from Government of India.\n- **Financial Benefit**: **Rs. 6,000 per year** transferred directly via Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) into Aadhaar-seeded bank accounts in **three equal four-monthly installments of Rs. 2,000 each**.\n- **Coverage**: Initially covered Small and Marginal Landholder Farmers (holding cultivable land up to 2 hectares); expanded from June 1, 2019 to cover **all landholding farmer families** irrespective of landholding size (subject to exclusion criteria like institutional landholders, constitutional post holders, income tax payees).",
