    "detailedExplanation": "### 1. Structural Significance of Services Sector in India\n- **GVA Contribution**: Over **53% of Gross Value Added (GVA)** at basic prices, generating over 30% of total national employment.\n- **FDI Inflows**: Attracts over 50% of total FDI equity inflows (Financial, Banking, Insurance, IT, R&D, Outsourcing).\n- **Export Dominance**: Services exports consistently run a substantial trade surplus (exceeding $150 billion net surplus), which critically offsets India's chronic merchandise trade deficit on the Current Account of the Balance of Payments.\n\n### 2. IT-BPM Sector & Digital Exports\n- **Pioneering Agencies**: Established via **Software Technology Parks of India (STPI)** in 1991 and liberal duty-free zones under the **Special Economic Zones (SEZ) Act, 2005**.\n- **Scale**: NASSCOM estimates Indian IT-BPM revenue above $250 billion, contributing ~7.5% of national GDP and employing over 5.4 million professionals directly.\n- **Global Delivery Model**: India holds over 55% market share of the global sourcing business, shifting from traditional low-cost maintenance to high-value Global Capability Centres (GCCs), Artificial Intelligence, Cloud Services, and Enterprise Software as a Service (SaaS).\n\n### 3. Financial Services, FinTech & Tourism\n- **FinTech Revolution**: India achieved the world's highest FinTech adoption rate (~87% vs global average ~64%), propelled by the India Stack (Aadhaar, e-KYC, UPI, Account Aggregator, DigiLocker).\n- **Tourism & Hospitality**: Contributes ~6-7% of GDP and significant employment; schemes like **PRASHAD** (Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual Augmentation Drive) and **SWADESH DARSHAN** promote integrated theme-based tourism circuits.\n\n### 4. Gig and Platform Economy: Labor Jurisprudence & Code on Social Security, 2020\n- **Definition of Gig Worker**: Under **Section 2(35) of the Code on Social Security, 2020**, a *'gig worker'* is defined as a person who performs work or participates in a work arrangement and earns from such activities outside of a traditional employer-employee relationship.\n- **Definition of Platform Worker**: Under **Section 2(61)**, a *'platform worker'* is defined as a person engaged in work arrangements wherein organizations use an online platform to access other organizations or individuals to solve specific problems or to provide specific services in exchange for payment.\n- **Social Security Fund (Section 141)**: Mandates the Central Government to establish a **Social Security Fund** for gig and platform workers. The fund is financed through a combination of contributions from the Central Government, State Governments, and an **aggregator cess** (between 1% to 2% of the annual turnover of aggregators like ride-hailing and food-delivery companies, not exceeding 5% of the amount paid to workers).\n- **National Social Security Board (Section 6)**: Formulated to recommend welfare schemes (life and disability cover, health and maternity benefits, old age protection, creches) for unorganized, gig, and platform workers.\n- **Judicial & Legislative Precedents**: Rajasthan passed the *Rajasthan Platform Based Gig Workers (Registration and Welfare) Act, 2023*, becoming the first Indian state to establish a dedicated regulatory welfare board and welfare cess on transactions.",
