    "introduction": "Foreign capital inflows into India are governed by the Foreign Exchange Management Act (FEMA), 1999 and the Consolidated FDI Policy formulated by the Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade (DPIIT). Inflows are bifurcated into Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) and Foreign Portfolio Investment (FPI), distinguished definitively by the Arvind Mayaram Committee report (2014) where investment >=10% of equity is classified as FDI and <10% as FPI. FDI enters either via the non-discretionary Automatic Route (requiring only ex-post notification to RBI) or the Government Approval Route (handled by relevant administrative ministries post-FIPB abolition). External trade is anchored by the Foreign Trade Policy (FTP) 2023, which abandoned arbitrary five-year cycles for a dynamic rolling horizon targeting $2 trillion in exports by 2030, supported by Special Economic Zones (SEZs) governed by the SEZ Act, 2005.",
